  "title": "Impact of built environment design on emotion measured via neurophysiological correlates and subjective indicators: A systematic review",
  "authors": "Isabella Bower et al., 2019",
  "review_question": "To establish if current research links the built environment to altered emotional states, how to evaluate interior built environment impacts on emotion, and if design can increase positive emotions for health and wellbeing.",
  "inclusion_exclusion_criteria": "Included studies where subjects were exposed to control and treatment conditions, reported ANS and/or CNS response in conjunction with self-report on emotional state, involved human participants (healthy and clinical populations), investigated design characteristics of the built environment (shape, color, symmetry, texture, form, scale, proportion, pattern, hierarchy) comparing intervention to neutral control/comparator, and used virtual (CAVE, VR HMD) or physically experienced (controlled conditions) interior settings. Excluded animal models, external/urban environments, studies with only subjective measures, or only IEQ variables.",
